# Task 5: End-to-End Individual Case Safety Report (ICSR) Assessment

## 1. Executive Summary & Four Core ICSR Elements
* **Identifiable Patient:** P. K., 32-year-old male, 68 kg.
* **Identifiable Reporter:** Healthcare Professional / Pharmacovigilance Associate.
* **Suspected Medicinal Product:** Amoxicillin 500 mg capsules.
* **Adverse Event (AE):** Drug-induced maculopapular cutaneous hypersensitivity reaction.
* **Case Validity:** Valid ICSR (meets all 4 mandatory regulatory criteria under ICH E2D).

---

## 2. Clinical Case Narrative
A 32-year-old male patient with no previous known drug allergies was prescribed oral Amoxicillin 500 mg TID for an acute upper respiratory tract bacterial infection. 

On Day 3 of therapy, the patient presented with generalized pruritus, followed by the eruption of an erythematous maculopapular rash across the trunk and upper extremities. Mucous membranes were unaffected, and there were no signs of respiratory distress, hemodynamic instability, or angioedema. 

Amoxicillin was immediately stopped (de-challenge). The patient received symptomatic supportive treatment with oral Cetirizine (10 mg OD). The rash resolved completely within 72 hours of drug cessation. No drug re-challenge was conducted.

